<code>assert !this.isEmpty() => (\result == null && this.getFirst() == null) || \result.equals(this.getFirst());</code>
--------------------
<code>assert this.isEmpty() => \result == null && this.getFirst() == null || !this.isEmpty() && \result == this.getFirst();</code>

However, given the method `getFirst()` throws a `NoSuchElementException` if the list is empty, the correct interpretation should be:

<code>assert !this.isEmpty() && \result == this.getFirst();</code>

This ensures that if the list is not empty, the result of `element()` is equal to the first element of the list. If the list were empty, `element()` would throw an exception, so we don't need to handle that case in the assert statement.
--------------------
<code>assert !this.isEmpty() => (\result == null && this.getFirst() == null) || \result == this.getFirst();</code>
--------------------
